1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,000
Yeah, I'm a bit of a weather buff.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
You know, you spend a lot of time outdoors, you go camping and things.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Well, you learn to watch the weather

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
And I wanted to talk to my Scouts and kind of teach them a little bit about cloud formations and you know weather systems and stuff like that,

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
And then I decided against it because it's all over their head.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,500
Okay, Yeah, you're right, It was that bad.

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,000
Hey, this is podcast number 368..

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
This is Clark Green

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And this is the last of our foundation series today

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And before we go into that, I have a couple of things to talk about.

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The first is a story that ended up being all over social media within the past week or so.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
You probably already know what I'm talking about because it was a scouting story.

15
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
It was a story about a special needs scout who was working towards Eagle

16
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And if you read the story, what had happened was all of his merit badges were taken away and he was demoted to being Cub Scout

17
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And his Eagle project was denied.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
It was just the BSA had done something just totally outrageous

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
And people were righteously outraged

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
And there was a lot of comment about it

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And back and forth. so much so that I actually heard from a Scouter friend of mine in Australia who had been discussing this with their fellow Scouters

22
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
because they had seen this article and they were like: what is going on with you people?

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:23,000
So, without going into a great deal of detail about the policy and procedural things that were misrepresented in this article,

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
whoever wrote this piece did it very irresponsibly, without much in the way of fact checking or research.

25
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The things that they reported that had happened were so far outside of anything that I understood about the BSA's handling of advancement-

26
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:49,000
even though I have arguments with the way they handle it- that I couldn't help but understand that this was an article that was specifically written just to outrage people

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
and to stir them up and to make them angry.

28
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
I've fallen for stuff like that before.

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
We probably all have-

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:05,000
And within a few days the BSA did make a very thorough statement about you know all the wise and wherefores of exactly what was going on

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:13,000
And, as you would assume, naturally they're supporting this special needs scout and their family to make sure that they have the opportunity to work towards Eagle.

32
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
And the one thing that I did, do you know, after I read this story I said: well, that doesn't sound right.

33
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Let me go look at the Guide to Advancement and make sure that I recall things correctly.

34
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And I looked and you know there's very specific ways of handling alternative requirements.

35
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
It's pretty clear cut way of doing it.

36
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
It's totally unambiguous,

37
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And so I figured somebody must have skipped a step somewhere along the way,

38
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And apparently that turns out to be the way that it happened.

39
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Big lesson from all this-

40
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
And I kind of feel like I learned this way back before social media, before the Internet- is when you read a new story and you're totally outraged by it,

41
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:55,000
you better check up and make sure that is not something that's designed to outrage you and make you upset and to react in a certain way,

42
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
especially in this instant response environment that we find ourselves in,

43
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
When something is that outrageous and just makes you livid and it feels like it's designed to make you react that way.

44
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:19,000
To me that's a signal to back off, to learn as much as I can about it if it's that important, and to be sure that I'm not just being provoked.

45
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
So it was an interesting example of what turned out to be a very irresponsible reporting.

46
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Anyway, I just saw that

47
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
I know a lot of you probably did and I couldn't help but to comment on it.

48
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
And speaking of comments and reactions, I got a few to last week.

49
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
I got quite a few to last week's podcast about developing leaders.

50
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:51,000
One of the things that had been part of all of our foundation series of podcasts is the constant reminder of the main aim of our work as scouters

51
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and the main aim of the scouting movement: the development of character in the individual scout.

52
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And last week I commented that the development of leadership skills is not our main aim.

53
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Making leaders is not our main aim.

54
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Our main aim is developing character,

55
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
And that didn't sit well with some people.

56
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And one comment that I got said this. I have to fundamentally disagree with you.

57
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Leaders may have good character or a bad character.

58
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Not everyone with good character or a bad character will ever really be a leader.

59
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:31,000
While an aim of scouting is growing good character, leadership is a separate aim and primary goal since the establishment of the program and the Eagle Rank

60
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Scouting teaches scouts to be leaders with many tenants that have little to do with character. although character development is a separate aim of the program,

61
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Leadership and leadership development is a very separate and specific objective for scouting.

62
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
It is heralded as such for over 100 years.

63
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Please do not try to water it down.

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Well, I will agree with you on this.

65
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Leadership skills are separate, specific and they're valuable.

66
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:08,000
But I will still tell you that their development, the development of leadership, is not the main aim or emphasis of scouting and it never has been.

67
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
It's not the main aim or emphasis of the Eagle Rank or any of the ranks in scouting, and it never has been.

68
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:25,000
It is certainly a percentage of those things, in the same way that camping skills and communication skills and all the other skill sets are a percentage of all of that,

69
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
But there is definitely not the main aim.

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
The main aim of our work as scouts is developing character, because it is the only source of good leadership.

71
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
I will definitely agree,

72
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
There are leaders of good character and leaders of bad character.

73
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
We don't have to look very far to find skillful leaders who lack character.

74
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Talking about character development as the main aim of scouting and the source of good leadership is not watering scouting down at all,

75
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Because the application of leadership skills, for good or for ill, is defined by the character of the individual.

76
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Good leadership is more than being a skilled manager or director.

77
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
It's clear that leadership and character are inseparable.

78
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Now, in last week's podcast, I talked about every scout exercising some form of leadership or another.

79
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:25,000
I break it down into four different kinds of leadership, and I don't think that that particular way of looking at it is original with me,

80
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
And I'm sure there are many others.

81
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:33,000
But I think these four different kinds of leadership are both interdependent and progressive,

82
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
And it all begins with that part of the scout in which we pledge to help other people at all times.

83
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
That's something that is not limited to those who are in roles of authority.

84
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
It's simply being responsible for the welfare of others,

85
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:53,000
And so if you need to be responsible for the welfare of others, you better start with yourself.

86
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Otherwise you're not going to be much good to others.

87
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
So leading yourself is one way of being a leader.

88
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Cooperatively following leaders and working as a member of a team is another way.

89
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Aiding and assisting your fellow scouts, being responsible for their well-being, is another way.

90
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:18,000
And then the fourth kind of leadership is the one that we usually think of first when we think of the term leadership,

91
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
That directive, managerial decision-making leadership.

92
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:29,000
So if we limit the definition of leadership to that fourth kind of leadership of authority over others or managing people-

93
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:37,000
and we focus on developing only those kinds of leadership skills, we're going to miss 99% of our opportunities as scouts,

94
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:46,000
And it would be a dangerous misservice to train leaders outside of the context of developing good character.

95
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And what I mean by that is leadership skills have vast potential for good or for bad.

96
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Think of it like this:

97
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,000
We teach our scouts to build fires, to light fires,

98
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
And that skill has a lot of potential,

99
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And that potential can be applied in a wise, helpful way,

100
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
And it can also be applied in a destructive, unhelpful way.

101
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:18,000
So when we teach the skills of how to light a fire, we also teach about the responsibilities that come along with that knowledge.

102
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:27,000
When we develop leadership skills in scouts, we also need to be conscious that we share with them

103
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,000
the importance of responsibly using those skills for the welfare of others.

104
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And that responsibility part- understanding the difference between good and bad- what is that?

105
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
That's character development.

106
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:49,000
So no, I'm not watering anything down, and developing leaders is not the main aim of advancement or anything about scouting.

107
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:56,000
And when we are working with scouts and developing leadership, it must always be done in the context of the development of character.

108
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Otherwise I think it's irresponsible.

109
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Well, as I said, we are at the last of our series on scouting foundations or fundamentals,

110
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And we're today we're going to talk about constructive discipline.

111
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
So let's get started, shall we?

112
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:55,000
One of the things that definitely occupies our mind as scouts,

113
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and one of the things that we definitely need to understand and be able to do, is to maintain discipline and require accountability and promote responsibility in our scouts.

114
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
And how do we do that,

115
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Since we're talking about foundations? it's very simple:

116
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Be an adult and be kind.

117
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:20,000
You know, in keeping with the way that we've examined other things about scouting in this series, let's ask ourselves: why do we need discipline?

118
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I know the answer is reasonably obvious.

119
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:31,000
There are a lot of things in the scout oath and law that point towards the idea of a disciplined approach to what we're doing.

120
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And, in the context of our analogy of scouting as a game, you know there's a lot of discipline that's exercised in playing a game.

121
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
You have to stay on the field,

122
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,000
You have to play fair,

123
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
You have to follow the rules of the game.

124
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:50,000
It's so woven into the fabric of the whole thing that it may seem strange to ask why, but I think it's important.

125
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:59,000
I'm not necessarily talking about discipline that results in quiet, compliant, obedient scouts 100% of the time.

126
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Now there are definitely instances where we absolutely require immediate compliance with our instructions,

127
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And those instances are focused on safety and propriety.

128
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:22,000
But we've got to look beyond the idea of just having the kind of discipline that an army officer demands of his soldiers, or a boss demands of his employees, or a teacher demands of their students.

129
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:30,000
This takes us right into the heart of the matter, the aim of scouting, which is the development of character in scouts.

130
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:39,000
When we're talking about constructive discipline, we're talking about the kind of discipline that is aimed at the growth of character,

131
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
And we don't grow character by being oppressive.

132
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
We don't grow character by being shaming.

133
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
We don't grow character by being harsh and demanding.

134
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:57,000
We grow character by asking the question: what is the right thing to do in this situation?

135
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Discipline is partially about shaping and reacting to the behavior of scouts.

136
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So let's talk about how we promote the main aim: the development of good character in scouts. when we're dealing with misbehavior,

137
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
It's very simple: be an adult and be kind.

138
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
And what do I mean by those two things?

139
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Be an ideal adult.

140
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:29,000
I have to deal with this at least as much and probably more than most people, because I'm kind of hot headed,

141
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
I'm reactive,

142
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
I understand that about myself.

143
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
I can go from zero to 60 pretty quick and I'm a big guy

144
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:41,000
And if I raise my voice I get what I want.

145
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:48,000
Now, in the face of impending disaster, that's a pretty good thing. as a response to just my being aggravated by the way scouts are behaving,

146
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Not a very good thing.

147
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:54,000
So when I'm saying, be an adult and be kind, those things have to be together right.

148
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
We can't just be a demanding, loud taskmaster,

149
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
We have to be kind at the same time.

150
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Part of that kindness is having enough compassion to understand that we were once in the same situation. the poor scout who's standing in front of us who's misbehaved was in.

151
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
So having a little compassion, being able to put yourself in their shoes and remember what it was like, is a good thing.

152
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Don't equate that with being permissive of bad behavior.

153
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:28,000
We do have to hold our scouts responsible for their actions when something has gone wrong and they've misbehaved,

154
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
But we can do it without shaming them.

155
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
We can do it without making them feel terrible and bad.

156
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
That's easy, right?

157
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
I mean, everybody knows how to do that.

158
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:44,000
We can just make them knuckle down, make them feel bad and send them away having learned pretty much nothing.

159
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Or we can be an adult and we can be kind and search for the application of the scout oath and law and apply it to the situation we find ourselves in.

160
00:14:53,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And whenever I talk about this, I really have to take a deep breath, because I am not good at this. every single time

161
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
I can be hotheaded and reactive.

162
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:12,000
I regret that, but in a lot of ways I'm doing the same work that my scouts are doing in trying to shape my reactions and my character to the scout oath and law.

163
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
You might be too,

164
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So we need to give ourselves a lot of breathing room when it comes to this sort of thing

165
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:25,000
And we need to focus on the constructive rather than destructive discipline.

166
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:32,000
I have a post on constructive discipline that goes in depth on a given situation as an example,

167
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,000
And I will have a link to that in the podcast notes.

168
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
It's worth going and taking a look at it,

169
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:44,000
But let's talk about some of the principles that are applied in that post without getting into too much detail,

170
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Because what we're looking at, we're talking about foundations here.

171
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:56,000
Applying constructive discipline is a skill that you have to develop in yourself

172
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
And, like any skill, it takes a little time.

173
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:00,000
It takes a lot of practice.

174
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
You have to be patient with yourself and you will get there.

175
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:18,000
And for the most part, our scouts are used to closed systems of rules and punishments and not an open-ended situation where they follow principles like the scout oath and law, rather than just a set of rules.

176
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:27,000
So, rather than issuing judgments about behavior, get scouts to look at their behavior and to judge it for themselves.

177
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
They'll catch the spirit of this.

178
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
It takes a little time and it takes consistency,

179
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
But they will catch the spirit of this.

180
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,000
I'm not a big fan of writing rules and regulations for scouts.

181
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,000
We don't need them.

182
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,000
We don't want them.

183
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
I've talked about policies and procedures and rules as parts of the game.

184
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
Yes, they're there,

185
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:57,000
But what I'm saying is is that don't sit down with the rest of the scouts in your troop and write a rule book or write a discipline policy or something like that.

186
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Scouts will find all kinds of ways around rules and regulations, especially when you take and put them on paper.

187
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:10,000
They cannot find a way around their own conscience. when it's motivated by the principles of the scout oath and law,

188
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:14,000
Rules and regulations are imposed impersonally on everyone.

189
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:20,000
The principles of the scout oath and law are adopted personally by individuals,

190
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
And when the scout oath and law are applied in this way,

191
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
scouts realize that they have practical meaning.

192
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:34,000
They're not just words we memorize and repeat because we like to hear the sound of our own voices.

193
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:39,000
The oath and law is more than an afterthought that we throw into this situation.

194
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,000
It's not just a decorative element of what we do as scouters.

195
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
It's not just something we repeat dryly during ceremonies.

196
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
It's the first thing we turn to in every situation.

197
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Another element of constructive discipline is the idea that we do not punish scouts who can't or won't follow the scout oath and law.

198
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Not your responsibility as a scouter,

199
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
It's the responsibility of the scouts family

200
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And it clarifies the responsibility that you have towards young people and that their parents have.

201
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Now, exactly how does that work,

202
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
You're asking. well, it's pretty simple.

203
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:28,000
When scouts behave badly, we differentiate between proportionate consequences and corrective punishment.

204
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:34,000
So proportionate consequences are just, you know, the direct result of the misbehavior.

205
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,000
If you've misused a knife or a saw or a hatchet,

206
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
well, a proportionate consequence would be not being able to do that

207
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:46,000
until you assured us that it was safe to allow you to use those things

208
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:52,000
If your misbehavior resulted in hurt feelings or some kind of a mess.

209
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,000
cleaning up the mess and making sure that we dealt with those hurt feelings

210
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:01,000
would be a part of proportionate consequences,

211
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:07,000
And anything beyond those proportionate consequences is corrective punishment.

212
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
Let me give you an example.

213
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:14,000
Let's stick with it with the use of knives and saws and axes and things like that.

214
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Now, if you have a rule book somewhere that says if you misuse a knife or saw or an axe,

215
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:25,000
you know you are going to be restrictive from using them for the next three months or something.

216
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:30,000
What you've done is you've instituted a corrective punishment,

217
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:35,000
trying to get to the heart of the matter, which is the misuse of the tool, right?

218
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:40,000
So if I'm walking through camp and I see a scout misusing his pocket knife,

219
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
I'm going to stop. I'm going to talk to the scout. I'm going to say:

220
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
do you understand you're misusing that

221
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
And we're going to have a discussion about it.

222
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:52,000
That is almost punishment enough, okay,

223
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,000
But really, honestly, we get them to tell us what they're doing wrong.

224
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
if they understand that it's wrong

225
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
And if not, well, we've corrected the situation

226
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:06,000
The second time that happens. okay, please close the knife and hand it to me.

227
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:09,000
I'll be keeping this for a little while because we talked about it before

228
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:15,000
and I want you to give this some thought and when you are ready to use this knife responsibly,

229
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
come and see me and you'll get it back.

230
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:21,000
Proportionate consequence. but I'm not going to take the knife away for six months

231
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
or three months or two weeks or seven hours. right,

232
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:31,000
I am going to engage the scout in figuring out exactly how to build their character

233
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:35,000
and how the scout of the law applies to this situation.

234
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Maybe the scout was doing something so outrageously bad

235
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
that I need to take the knife and then give it to their parent.

236
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
and I need to say to their parent: look, this is what happened.

237
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,000
here's their knife. you need to talk about this

238
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:52,000
because you need to assure me that something like this is never going to happen again.

239
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:55,000
And then it's up to the parent to decide how to handle that

240
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,000
and they may have a corrective punishment that they're going to apply.

241
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
but it's not my job.

242
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,000
We don't want to get into the world of corrective punishment

243
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and we definitely don't want our youth leaders to be in that world.

244
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:13,000
So corrective punishments like oh, demerits

245
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:17,000
or some kind of forced physical activity like doing push-ups or something.

246
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
They're completely out of place.

247
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
They're not something that you want to be involved in

248
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and they're not something that you should allow your scouts to do

249
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
or allow any adult to do to a scout.

250
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
That is not your responsibility and not your place.

251
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:36,000
That is a parent's prerogative and a parent's responsibility.

252
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:40,000
Now, if we can't resolve everything with constructive discipline,

253
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:44,000
proportionate consequences, counseling and talking with scouts,

254
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
then it's time for their parents to take over.

255
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:47,000
It's that simple.

256
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Now you might be thinking: well, it's not really that simple,

257
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
because I have 40 scouts in a troop

258
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
and we have to have some kind of rules.

259
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
We have some way of handling this giant mess.

260
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Well, you already have a set of rules.

261
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,000
You already have a way that good behavior is defined.

262
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
It's called the Scout Oath and Law

263
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and that's where you turn in every situation.

264
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
It's the first thing that you go to.

265
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Now, is this more difficult and complicated

266
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
than having a set of rules about common misbehaviors

267
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
that also describe corrective punishments

268
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:20,000
and stuff like that?

269
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:21,000
Of course it is,

270
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
But welcome to the game.

271
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
This is what scouting does

272
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:28,000
because, remember, rules are about compelling good behavior

273
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
on the part of everyone involved.

274
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,000
That does not aid in the development of individual character,

275
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
because when scouts misbehave,

276
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:40,000
when we need to apply constructive discipline,

277
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
it's a tremendous opportunity for us.

278
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:46,000
It's a tremendous opportunity to advance

279
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,000
towards the main aim: the development of character in our scouts.

280
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:53,000
And good group behavior comes from good individual behavior.

281
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:58,000
So we want to aim our effort at developing discipline in individuals.

282
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:02,000
Speaking to scouts individually opens a dialogue

283
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,000
that helps them understand and develop an internal standard

284
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
based on the Scout Oath and Law.

285
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,000
I'll tell you this for nothing, while we're on the subject,

286
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:14,000
What you talk about with your scouts as a group

287
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:18,000
is the kind of behavior that you're going to see repeated.

288
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:21,000
Now, if you give negative attention to an individual scout

289
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:24,000
about misbehavior in front of a group of scouts,

290
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
you will probably have the opportunity to do that again

291
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:29,000
because you've just paid attention to a scout individually

292
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:32,000
about a way that they misbehave.

293
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
If you remove the scout from the situation

294
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
and talk to them individually about it,

295
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:39,000
naturally following all of the youth protection guidelines-

296
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
when you do that, you're going to find that A

297
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
it's much more effective for the scout

298
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
and it is a clear message to the group.

299
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
They don't have to be told what's going on.

300
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
And the inverse of this is true

301
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
When you see good behavior

302
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:55,000
when you see something that you want to see repeated

303
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:58,000
now, that's when you mention it to the whole group.

304
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
Somebody goes out of their way to be kind,

305
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:03,000
when somebody goes out of their way to help other people,

306
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:06,000
when somebody goes out of their way to demonstrate

307
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
any point of the scout oath and law,

308
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
jump on that and you want to make sure

309
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
that all of your scouts understand

310
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
that this is a good thing

311
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and this is something that you really appreciate.

312
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
You'll see that repeated.

313
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
If you speak about the problems that you're having

314
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,000
with individuals, with all of your scouts,

315
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
those problems will likely multiply.

316
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
If you speak about positive actions,

317
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,000
those positive actions are going to multiply.

318
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:34,000
Scouting discipline is relentlessly positive.

319
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
We want to maintain a positive attitude

320
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,000
and confidence in scouts and being able

321
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
to build their character and evidence

322
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
that character and their behavior.

323
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:46,000
We need to make sure that we do not equate

324
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:49,000
bad behavior with being a bad person.

325
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:52,000
We want to have some empathy and some compassion

326
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
for the frustration and conflict that scouts

327
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
are feeling as they figure these things out

328
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
and they build their character.

329
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
And when we're talking about being an adult

330
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
and being kind, the attitude that you get back

331
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
when you step into a situation to correct

332
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
a bad behavior

333
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
will not usually be welcoming

334
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
and saying:

335
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:14,000
I really appreciate you helping me go through this.

336
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
It will be resentful

337
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
and leave me alone.

338
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:21,000
You experienced this with your own children.

339
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:24,000
In reality, very much of the time

340
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
they appreciate that you have stepped in

341
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:29,000
because, A- it evidences that you care for them

342
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:32,000
And, B- they're often caught in a loop

343
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,000
of bad behavior that they sense is not right,

344
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:38,000
but they really can't figure out what to do with it.

345
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
So being an adult having broader experience

346
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:44,000
and knowledge than our scouts do

347
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:47,000
is a really important factor in this

348
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:51,000
Discipline and scouting is also a cooperative effort.

349
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
Our youth leaders may be able to resolve

350
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,000
smaller discipline-related problems on their own,

351
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
but they should be instructed

352
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:02,000
to bring any behavior problem to our attention.

353
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:05,000
They may have resolved things already.

354
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
We should be aware of precisely

355
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
how they reach that resolution,

356
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
And when I talk to my youth leaders

357
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
and we talk about how things are going,

358
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
we talk about it in specifics.

359
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
Did you have to correct anybody?

360
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
talk to anybody, discipline anybody about anything?

361
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
Can you tell me how that went

362
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
and tell me exactly what you did?

363
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
Because it's our responsibility to make sure

364
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
that they're applying their leadership skills

365
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,000
in the same way that we strive to apply them right,

366
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:34,000
With kindness, understanding, compassion and empathy.

367
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:38,000
And finally, I'll say as a practical point

368
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:42,000
that emotional scouts are not very good counselors

369
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:46,000
and they are not very good disciplinarians.

370
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:49,000
If you're like me and you're a little hot-headed

371
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:52,000
and reactive, or a lot hot-headed and reactive,

372
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,000
be aware of that and make sure that before you act

373
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:59,000
you have gauged your reaction with a fellow scouter. perhaps

374
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:02,000
We're never at our best when our emotions are running high

375
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
and misbehavior in our scouts

376
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,000
can be really frustrating and upsetting.

377
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:10,000
Check in with a fellow scouter, gauge your level of emotion

378
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:13,000
and don't react until you're level-headed.

379
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:16,000
So just to review discipline- and scouting

380
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
is supposed to be constructive.

381
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,000
The constructive part is the building of character,

382
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:25,000
the first reference point. the last reference point:

383
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
Every single rule of behavior that we need

384
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:31,000
is already there, embodied in the Scout Oath and Law.

385
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
It should be our expectation,

386
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
It should be our goal that all our scouts

387
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
are developing their character

388
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
and evidencing that in their behavior

389
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:43,000
by their adherence to the Scout Oath and Law.

390
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,000
In applying constructive discipline,

391
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:48,000
we're always adults and we're always kind.

392
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
We strive to be level-headed,

393
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:53,000
We strive to be compassionate, empathetic

394
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:56,000
and understanding of the position that our scouts are in

395
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:00,000
Being too authoritarian and demanding

396
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:03,000
is as bad as being overly permissive,

397
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:06,000
striking the balance. take some practice,

398
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
take some skill,

399
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and it's something to think about

400
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:13,000
and to concentrate on in your work as a scouter.

401
